create-cluster is a small script used to easily start a big number of Redis
instances configured to run in cluster mode. Its main goal is to allow manual
testing in a condition which is not easy to replicate with the Redis cluster
unit tests, for example when a lot of instances are needed in order to trigger
a given bug.

The tool can also be used just to easily create a number of instances in a
Redis Cluster in order to experiment a bit with the system.

USAGE
---

To start a temporary Redis Cluster:

1. Build redis-server and redis-cli (run "make" in the repository root).
   Optionally override settings such as PORT and NODES in config.sh in the
   current directory. The defaults use six nodes on ports 30001-30006
   (cluster bus ports 40001-40006).
2. Run "./create-cluster start-and-create".

This starts the Redis instances, waits for them to become ready, and creates
the cluster automatically without prompting for confirmation.

The command stays running until Ctrl+C is pressed. It then stops the instances
and removes the temporary data, configuration files, and logs. SIGTERM and
startup or cluster creation failures also trigger cleanup. Temporary files
are placed under TMPDIR (or /tmp). Cluster creation has a 60-second deadline.
Cleanup force-stops unresponsive child processes after a short grace period.
If cleanup fails, it returns an error and may leave temporary files behind.

ADDITIONAL_OPTIONS in config.sh accepts space-separated Redis options, as with
the manual commands. The temporary command always manages the node ports,
data paths, and foreground process lifetime itself. Options requiring client
authentication or TLS are not supported by its automatic readiness checks.

(If you're accessing your setup via a local container, ensure that the
CLUSTER_HOST value is changed to your local IP.)

Alternatively, you can manage the cluster manually:

1. Use "./create-cluster start" to start the instances.
2. Use "./create-cluster create" to create the cluster. Add "-f" to skip
   the confirmation prompt.
3. Use "./create-cluster stop" to stop the instances.
4. Use "./create-cluster clean" to remove the generated data and logs.

The manual commands preserve the instances' files in the current directory,
allowing the cluster to be stopped and restarted without losing its data.

Use the command "./create-cluster help" to get the full list of features.
